L(500, "- **(a)**", "- **(a)** Scope and order for the native arm: whole projects only, in the plan's order. At NP1b a project is in scope if and only if it passes NP1.2's inputs test on the study's source manifest. One that fails is \"not planned (NP1b: inputs)\". No usage reading, number of weeks or quality value decides scope at NP1b. AI_READI can also leave when its pilot concludes without acceptance; a withheld word removes nothing, and neither does an accepted pilot with no usable fraction (NP1.2, NP1.6). *Changed: one manifest.*")
L(502, "- **(c)**", "- **(c)** Kids First is not planned under this amendment. It has no native cell here and is labelled \"not planned (NP1)\", which is not missing. Its inputs sit under their own manifest with the neutral profile, the plan's `vocabulary: {}` cannot be written in a native registration, and it is the largest input and unpriced. A later addendum can add it once its inputs test is specified. A no returns this item for redrafting (NP1.2). *Rewritten: v7 had it in scope if its own manifest passed.*")
L(503, "- **(d)**", "- **(d)** VOICE_PEDIATRIC's descriptive cell is a production cell: it runs under the registered floor and under NP1.6's acceptance rows, attempt bound, classification and stop-loss, and it never enters K_native or a verdict (NP1.2). *Changed: one descriptive cell.*")
L(504, "- **(e)**", "- **(e)** One native AI_READI pilot, not a replicate, with the floor pending, accepted before the floor's value is computed. A no means the floor comes from the CHORUS reference run alone, and AI_READI's first run is its first replicate (NP1.2). *Reworded.*")
L(505, "- **(f)**", "- **(f)** Every pilot has at most two attempts and concludes by NP1.6's table, with the consequence that table gives when a pilot concludes without acceptance. With no further word a pilot stays pending, and the floor is not registered. No pilot is run again: a pilot's reading is never replaced by a second run of that pilot (NP1.6). *Changed: no re-run.*")
L(507, "- **(h)**", "- **(h)** D113's \"native shared-v1 cell only\" is read as the whole native shared-v1 condition: after a rule-level rejection of the canary this amendment has nothing to apply to. Whether a canary ending is rule-level is read by the plan's precedence, and a later accepted canary attempt does not undo it. A rule-level rejection of the CHORUS reference pilot has the same consequence as the canary's. A no keeps the narrow reading, under which a later prospective amendment could add a native CHORUS pilot in this condition (NP1.6). *Changed: the reference pilot.*")
L(517, "- **(n)**", "- **(n)** **Answered: keep (D141).** The incomplete capture retains the current full and core records with their sha256, as an engineering prerequisite of any pilot, so that a stopped or rejected attempt can have an eligible record. It depends on (m). The lead's condition, which NP1b confirms on the merged change: the retained files are members of the capture's snapshot. The alternative that was offered, and not taken: the native eligible set limited to attempts whose recorder ran (NP1.4). *The lead's condition is marked.*")
L(523, "- **(r)**", "- **(r)** **Answered: at-receipt (D140).** The floor's basis is D37's at-receipt coverage. A registered numeric floor declares `basis: receipted_part`, and one without it is refused; a pending registration is unchanged and may carry the at-receipt counters as a diagnostic reading (D140, with the lead's ruling on its form). The lead's addition: the code change is in the source the canary's registration pins. The basis does not change afterwards in this condition. The alternative that was offered, and not taken: the final-record basis (NP1.5). *The lead's addition is marked.*")
L(524, "- **(s)**", "- **(s)** The floor rule: the lowest of the eligible native pilots' at-receipt fractions, each read once from the final-stage check, rounded down to a multiple of 1/20 and capped at 19/20. The eligible pilots are the CHORUS reference run and the AI_READI pilot. The lowest value that can be registered is 1/20. The floor is not registered, and no production cell registers: while a required pilot is pending; while an instrument finding on the two readings is open; while a pilot's comparison cannot be decided; where an accepted pilot has no usable fraction, in which case its project stays in scope and a prospective amendment says how the floor is set; and where there is no reference run (NP1.5). *Changed: every case is listed.*")
L(528, "- **(u)**", "- **(u)** N1.4(B)'s rows B0–B7 judge the AI_READI pilot, the CHORUS reference pilot and every production cell, with B0 read for production as the receipts gate under the structural floors and the registered floor (NP1.6). *Changed: the reference pilot.*")
L(535, "- **(z)**", "- **(z)** The staged procedure: no pilot or production registration under this amendment before the canary's acceptance record is signed; B6's reading of every canary attempt that kept a record before NP1b; then the measured-usage packet, then NP1b, then the CHORUS reference pilot where the canary cannot be the reference run, then the AI_READI pilot, then the floor's registration, each reviewed and ratified before the registrations that depend on it. NP1b applies rules, changes none and takes no number from the owner; computing the floor by (s) is an application (NP1.5, NP1.7). *Changed: no comparison step and no re-run.*")
L(537, "- **(ab)**", "- **(ab)** **Answered: B (D143).** Runtime terms. **B:** two registered sets and nothing else: the canary keeps amendment N1's $90 guard and 21,600 s deadline, and every later pilot and every production cell runs under one production set, a $400 guard and a 43,200 s deadline. Because the limits are content the model reads, under B the canary is not the reference run, and one CHORUS reference pilot under the production set supplies the reference and CHORUS's floor input. **A,** which the lead recommended after that was found and which the owner had not answered when this draft was written: that one set for every run, the canary included. The canary is then the reference run if it is accepted and (aj)'s condition held, and amendment N1 is revised in those two values and balloted again. **C,** offered and not taken: the canary's terms for every run. The lead's addition to D143: no project is named in code. The answer on record at ratification is the one in force (NP1.5, NP1.7). *Changed: what B costs, and the lead's recommendation of A.*")
L(538, "- **(ac)**", "- **(ac)** The admission bounds, overage disabled, the freshness terms, the keep-awake policy, the capture rules, the context mode, the quiet account and the two-week validity of a registration, each decided or ratified for the canary, apply unchanged to every pilot and production attempt. One attempt at a time follows from the quiet account. The guard and the deadline are (ab)'s (NP1.7). *Changed: the validity term.*")
L(539, "- **(ad)**", "- **(ad)** One procedure: every pilot after the reference run and every production cell is registered equal to the reference by (al)'s comparison. A change that the comparison finds not equal, which a change to admission, retry, stopping, selection or retention always is, ends this stratum for the runs registered after it and needs its own prospective amendment; it never moves this stratum's floor or K_native. A change that it finds equal stays in the stratum on D35's terms: the comparison, and one diagnostic canary that the owner separately authorizes (NP1.7). *Changed: the reference in place of the pinned source.*")
L(549, "- **(aj)**", "- **(aj)** Canary first: the canary's coverage reading can be a floor input, and the canary can be the reference run, only if this amendment was ratified, by this file's sha256 with a recorded answer to every item, before the canary's first coded word. That is judged once, at that word, and nothing later changes it: a later change to this amendment applies only to cells registered after it. Where the condition failed, the canary's reading is never a floor input and the canary is not the reference run; the CHORUS reference pilot supplies both under (al). A no means the same, and nothing is redrafted (NP1.5, NP1.6, NP1.7). *Changed: the reference pilot in place of a re-run.*")
L(552, "- **(ak)**", "- **(ak)** What a \"no\" means: it returns the item for redrafting, except for the ten items that carry a written alternative; a redraft is ratified before the first registration whose outcome it governs, as the table above names it; and production registrations wait for every unresolved analysis-defining item (NP1.10). *Changed: the bar on native results is now (ao).*")
L(554, "**Source and starts**", "**Reference and starts**")
L(555, "- **(al)**", "- **(al)** The reference run and the comparison. The production stratum's reference is the first accepted CHORUS pilot attempt that ran under the production limit set and whose first coded word followed this amendment's ratification: the N1 canary under one limit set where (aj)'s condition held, and otherwise the first accepted attempt of the CHORUS reference pilot, one CHORUS pilot registered after NP1b under the production limit set with the floor pending. The reference is everything that run's registration pinned, the binary, the system prompt and the bounds rule included. The AI_READI pilot's registration and every production registration are equal to it by NP1.5's comparison, a script with a data file pinned by sha256, or they are not in this stratum. \"Cannot decide\" is a hold, any difference in a rule-bearing object is \"not equal\", and no reviewer judges a difference. No pilot is run again, and a change that is not equal ends the stratum for the runs registered after it (NP1.5, NP1.6, NP1.7). *Rewritten: it replaces v7's pinned source, two-part test and re-run pilots.*")
L(556, "- **(am)**", """
- **(am)** **Answered: allowed, for production runs (D145).** After giving a production attempt's word, the owner may direct the lead to start that attempt's sequence, within the limits of amendment N1's items (z) and (aa), the direction naming that attempt's registration. Every attempt still needs its own word (D22) (NP1.7). *Changed: production runs only, as D145 says.*
- **(an)** Delegated starts for this amendment's pilots, the AI_READI pilot and the CHORUS reference pilot, on the same terms as (am). D145 names production runs, and amendment N1's item (aa) covers the canary; neither names these pilots. A no leaves the owner to start those sequences (NP1.7). *New.*

**Use of results**
- **(ao)** No change may rest on a native result. From the canary's first coded word until the last in-scope production cell's acceptance record is signed, or until the native shared-v1 condition ends, no coverage fraction, rubric score, review verdict, other quality value or content of a native record is cited or relied on as a reason for a change to this amendment, a change of source, asset, binary or registered value, the timing of either, or a comparison's disposition. A change may cite a filed defect, the class of a failed attempt and the case of NP1.5 or NP1.6 that occurred, and a ratified prerequisite. A new stratum's amendment is bound by the same bar (NP1.5). *New: v7 had it inside (ak), undefined.*
""")

